  abstract = {Masked priming effects for prefixed words sharing a bound stem (e.g., submit-PERMIT) are compared with priming effects for semantically transparent prefixed words (e.g., fold-UNFOLD). Exp 1 tested whether prefixed words sharing the same bound stem show priming effects in a masked priming paradigm using 54 Ss. Exp 2 tested whether the priming observed for bound stem items was any greater than would be expected on the basis of orthographic overlap alone using 56 Ss. Exp 3 attempted to suppress the form effect by using close distractors and increasing the prime duration with 30 Ss. The results of these experiments demonstrate three points. Morphological priming effects with prefixed words can be obtained when primes are masked despite semantic opacity. Such priming depends on productivity, or family size. Morphological priming effects of none appear to summate with orthographic effects. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2005 APA, all rights reserved).},
